From: Cloud Kernel BBRv3 Maintainers <noreply@cloud-kernel-bbrv3.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] compat: replace strncpy() which Linux 7.2 removed

Linux 7.2 dropped strncpy() from the kernel API after a six-year
deprecation cycle, so out-of-tree users fail to compile with:

  netlink.c:175:9: error: implicit declaration of function 'strncpy'

Both call sites copy a length-checked substring and terminate it
manually, which is exactly what memcpy() is for, and that works on
every kernel series this project builds.

---
 netlink.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/netlink.c b/netlink.c
index b1da877..0901a18 100644
--- a/netlink.c
+++ b/netlink.c
@@ -172,7 +172,7 @@ static inline int parse_ipv4_prefix(const char *prefix_str, struct ipv4_prefix *
 	if (slash - prefix_str >= INET_ADDRSTRLEN)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	strncpy(addr_str, prefix_str, slash - prefix_str);
+	memcpy(addr_str, prefix_str, slash - prefix_str);
 	addr_str[slash - prefix_str] = '\0';
 
 	ret = kstrtoint(slash + 1, 10, &prefix->prefix_len);
@@ -231,7 +231,7 @@ static inline int parse_ipv6_prefix(const char *prefix_str, struct ipv6_prefix *
 	if (slash - prefix_str >= INET6_ADDRSTRLEN)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	strncpy(addr_str, prefix_str, slash - prefix_str);
+	memcpy(addr_str, prefix_str, slash - prefix_str);
 	addr_str[slash - prefix_str] = '\0';
 
 	ret = kstrtoint(slash + 1, 10, &prefix->prefix_len);
